# SORT(1)

## NAME

sort - sort or merge files

## SYNOPSIS

| **sort** [ **-mubdfinrt**\&*x* ] [ **+**\&*pos1* [ **-**\&*pos2* ] ] ... [ **-o** name ] [ **-T** directory ] [ name ] ...

## DESCRIPTION

*Sort* sorts lines of all the named files together and writes the result on the standard
output. The name `-` means the standard input. If no input files are named, the standard input
is sorted.

The default sort key is an entire line. Default ordering is lexicographic by bytes in machine
collating sequence. The ordering is affected globally by the following options, one or more of
which may appear.

**b**
: Ignore leading blanks (spaces and tabs) in field comparisons.

**d**
: `Dictionary` order: only letters, digits and blanks are significant in comparisons. *Note:* a
  byte above 0177 is significant too. Text on this machine is UTF-8, so the two bytes of a
  Cyrillic letter are two thirds of a letter and not punctuation. v7's table ignored every byte
  of 0200-0377, which made *привет* and *мир* compare equal and left the order between
  them to whatever the sort happened to do.

**f**
: Fold upper case letters onto lower case. *Note:* the table folds lower onto UPPER, which this
  page has always had backwards; the direction is not cosmetic, since it decides where a letter
  sorts relative to the punctuation lying between the two cases. A byte above 0177 has no case
  and is left alone. v7's table also folded **]** onto **\\**, a slip in a hand-written table,
  corrected here.

**i**
: Ignore characters outside the ASCII range 040-0176 in nonnumeric comparisons. *Note:* tab and
  newline are not ignored - the comparison stops at the newline it can see, so a table that
  dropped it would walk into the next line - and neither is a byte above 0177, for the reason
  **d** gives.

**n**
: An initial numeric string, consisting of optional blanks, optional minus sign, and zero or more
  digits with optional decimal point, is sorted by arithmetic value. Option **n** implies option
  **b.**

**r**
: Reverse the sense of comparisons.

**t**\&*x*
: `Tab character` separating fields is *x*.

The notation **+**\&*pos1* **-**\&*pos2* restricts a sort key to a field beginning at *pos1*
and ending just before *pos2*. *Pos1* and *pos2* each have the form *m*\&**.**\&*n*, optionally
followed by one or more of the flags **bdfinr,** where *m* tells a number of fields to skip
from the beginning of the line and *n* tells a number of characters to skip further. If any
flags are present they override all the global ordering options for this key. If the **b**
option is in effect *n* is counted from the first nonblank in the field; **b** is attached
independently to *pos2*. A missing **.**\&*n* means .0; a missing **-**\&*pos2* means the end
of the line. Under the **-t**\&*x* option, fields are strings separated by *x*; otherwise
fields are nonempty nonblank strings separated by blanks.

When there are multiple sort keys, later keys are compared only after all earlier keys compare
equal. Lines that otherwise compare equal are ordered with all bytes significant - except under
**-u**, where equal keys are equal lines and the tie is not broken. Which member of a set of
equal lines survives **-u** is not defined.

These option arguments are also understood:

**c**
: Check that the input file is sorted according to the ordering rules; give no output unless the
  file is out of sort.

**m**
: Merge only, the input files are already sorted.

**o**
: The next argument is the name of an output file to use instead of the standard output. This
  file may be the same as one of the inputs.

**T**
: The next argument is the name of a directory in which temporary files should be made. *Note:*
  it replaces */tmp* rather than being tried ahead of it, so a directory named here and unusable
  is a diagnostic rather than a silent fall-back. v7 tried */usr/tmp* first and */tmp* second;
  this system has no */usr/tmp* and the first try was a wasted creat(2) on every run.

**u**
: Suppress all but one in each set of equal lines. Ignored bytes and bytes outside keys do not
  participate in this comparison.

**Examples.** Print in alphabetical order all the unique spellings in a list of words.
Capitalized words differ from uncapitalized.

sort -u +0f +0 list

Print the password file (passwd(5)) sorted by user id number (the 3rd colon-separated field).

sort -t: +2n /etc/passwd

Print the first instance of each month in an already sorted file of (month day) entries. The
options **-um** with just one input file make the choice of a unique representative from a set
of equal lines predictable.

sort -um +0 -1 dates

## FILES

/tmp/stm?????aa - /tmp/stm?????zz: the temporary files, made as they are needed and unlinked
before *sort* exits. There are at most 676 of them.

## SEE ALSO

uniq(1), comm(1), rev(1), join(1)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Comments and exits with nonzero status for various trouble conditions and for disorder
discovered under option **-c**.

## BLOCKS AND LINES

A line may be 3072 bytes long, terminator included. A longer one is a diagnostic and a nonzero
exit, on the sorting pass and on the merging pass alike. v7 truncated it, silently, and only
when a merge happened to be involved: the sorting pass had no limit at all, so the same file
came out right or came out corrupted depending on whether it fit in memory in one pass.

## BUGS

None known. The v7 page said `very long lines are silently truncated`; see BLOCKS AND LINES
above for what happens instead.
